---
import BlogLayout from '../../layouts/BlogLayout.astro';

const bodyContent = `<p>A single AI agent can answer questions. A pipeline of specialized agents can solve problems. The challenge is connecting them: finding the right agents, moving data between them, handling failures, and doing it all securely. This article shows how to build multi-agent pipelines using OpenClaw agents connected over Pilot Protocol encrypted tunnels -- from simple two-agent chains to complex DAG workflows with parallel branches and conditional routing.</p>

  <section>
  <h2 id="pipeline-problem">The Pipeline Problem</h2>

  <p>Modern AI workflows chain multiple models and tools. A typical analysis pipeline might look like:</p>

  <ol>
    <li><strong>Data Ingestion Agent</strong> -- fetches raw data from sources</li>
    <li><strong>Preprocessing Agent</strong> -- cleans, normalizes, validates</li>
    <li><strong>Analysis Agent</strong> -- runs ML models or statistical analysis</li>
    <li><strong>Reporting Agent</strong> -- formats results into human-readable output</li>
  </ol>

  <p>Each agent is an OpenClaw instance running on potentially different hardware, in different locations, behind different NATs. The preprocessing agent might need fast SSDs. The analysis agent might need a GPU. The reporting agent might need access to a template rendering service.</p>

  <p>The standard approach is HTTP APIs between each stage. This requires public endpoints (or VPN), TLS certificate management, retry logic, and circuit breakers at every hop. For 4 agents, that is 3 HTTP integrations to build and maintain.</p>

  <p>With Pilot Protocol, the agents just trust each other and communicate. No HTTP servers. No TLS certificates. No VPN. The tunnel handles encryption, NAT traversal, and reliable delivery.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
  <h2 id="two-agent">Two-Agent Chain</h2>

  <p>The simplest pipeline: one agent sends work to another and waits for results.</p>

  <pre><code><span class="comment"># Agent A: Send file and work description to Agent B (hostname: preprocessor)</span>
<span class="cmd">pilotctl send-file preprocessor ./raw-data.csv</span>
<span class="cmd">pilotctl send-message preprocessor --wait 10m --data \\
  '{"description":"Clean and normalize the CSV: remove nulls, standardize dates, convert currencies to USD","params":{"input":"raw-data.csv","output_format":"parquet"}}'</span>
<span class="comment"># --wait prints Agent B's reply inline: the result JSON with the cleaned data location.
# Agent B answers with: pilotctl send-message &lt;agent-a&gt; --reply-to &lt;message_id&gt; --data '...'</span>

<span class="comment"># A reply that arrives after the wait is still saved to the inbox</span>
<span class="cmd">pilotctl --json inbox --from preprocessor --latest</span></code></pre>

  <p>Agent A blocks waiting for the reply. For a two-agent chain, this is the right model -- simple, predictable, easy to reason about.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
  <h2 id="fan-out">Fan-Out: Parallel Branches</h2>

  <p>When independent tasks can run simultaneously, fan out to multiple agents in parallel:</p>

  <pre><code><span class="comment"># Orchestrator fans out to three specialists in parallel</span>
<span class="comment"># (In practice, the orchestrator spawns these as concurrent tool calls)</span>

<span class="comment"># Branch 1: Sentiment analysis</span>
<span class="cmd">pilotctl send-message sentiment --wait 5m --data \\
  '{"description":"Run sentiment analysis on customer reviews","params":{"model":"roberta-sentiment"}}' &amp;</span>

<span class="comment"># Branch 2: Topic extraction</span>
<span class="cmd">pilotctl send-message topics --wait 5m --data \\
  '{"description":"Extract topics from customer reviews using LDA","params":{"num_topics":"10"}}' &amp;</span>

<span class="comment"># Branch 3: Named entity recognition</span>
<span class="cmd">pilotctl send-message ner --wait 5m --data \\
  '{"description":"Extract named entities: products, companies, people"}' &amp;</span>

<span class="comment"># Each command prints its branch's reply when it arrives; wait for all three</span>
<span class="cmd">wait</span></code></pre>

  <p>The orchestrator sends all three tasks simultaneously. Each specialist works independently on its branch. When all three complete, the orchestrator merges the results. This is the MapReduce pattern applied to agent pipelines.</p>

  <p>For fan-out to work well, the orchestrator needs to handle partial failures. If the sentiment agent fails but topic extraction and NER succeed, the orchestrator should still produce a partial result rather than failing entirely.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
  <h2 id="event-driven">Event-Driven Pipelines</h2>

  <p>For pipelines where stages trigger asynchronously, use Pilot Protocol's pub/sub event stream. Every daemon runs an event stream on port 1002; the stages below all publish and subscribe on one node, named <code>coordinator</code>:</p>

  <pre><code><span class="comment"># Stage 1: Data agent publishes when new data arrives</span>
<span class="cmd">pilotctl publish coordinator pipeline.data.ready \\
  --data '{"source": "api-feed", "rows": 15000, "path": "/data/batch-42.parquet"}'</span>

<span class="comment"># Stage 2: Preprocessing agent subscribes and auto-triggers</span>
<span class="cmd">pilotctl subscribe coordinator pipeline.data.ready</span>
<span class="comment"># When event arrives, agent starts cleaning the data</span>

<span class="comment"># Stage 3: Analysis agent subscribes to preprocessed data</span>
<span class="cmd">pilotctl subscribe coordinator pipeline.preprocessed.ready</span>

<span class="comment"># Each stage publishes completion events that trigger the next stage</span></code></pre>

  <p>The event-driven model decouples pipeline stages. No stage needs to know the address of the next stage -- it just publishes a completion event. Any agent subscribed to that topic picks up the work. This enables dynamic scaling: if the preprocessing stage is slow, spin up a second preprocessing agent subscribed to the same topic. Both agents receive every data-ready event, so give them a rule for which batches each one takes (for example, odd and even batch numbers).</p>
  </section>

  <section>
  <h2 id="conditional">Conditional Routing</h2>

  <p>Some pipelines need branching logic: if the data contains anomalies, route to the anomaly investigation agent. If the data is clean, route directly to analysis.</p>

  <pre><code><span class="comment"># Preprocessing agent evaluates the data and routes conditionally</span>
<span class="cmd">pilotctl publish coordinator pipeline.preprocessed.anomaly \\
  --data '{"anomaly_count": 47, "severity": "high"}'</span>

<span class="comment"># OR, if data is clean:</span>
<span class="cmd">pilotctl publish coordinator pipeline.preprocessed.clean \\
  --data '{"rows": 14953, "path": "/data/clean-42.parquet"}'</span>

<span class="comment"># Different agents subscribe to different topics:
# Anomaly investigator: pipeline.preprocessed.anomaly
# Standard analyzer: pipeline.preprocessed.clean</span></code></pre>

  <p>The routing logic lives in the publishing agent. It evaluates the data and publishes to the appropriate topic. Downstream agents do not need conditional logic -- they subscribe to their specific topic and process whatever arrives.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
  <h2 id="discovery">Dynamic Agent Discovery</h2>

  <p>Hard-coded hostnames work for fixed pipelines. For dynamic pipelines, put the agents in a private network and pick the best available agent at runtime by hostname (name your GPU trainers with a recognizable prefix). Networks are created by the registry operator, so this assumes you run your own rendezvous and have added the agents to network 7:</p>

  <pre><code><span class="comment"># List members of the network, then pick a gpu-trainer by hostname</span>
<span class="cmd">pilotctl --json network members 7</span>
<span class="comment"># data.nodes: [{"address":"7:0007.0C11.2A33","hostname":"gpu-trainer-1",...},
#              {"address":"7:0007.0C11.5D66","hostname":"gpu-trainer-2",...}]</span>

<span class="comment"># Send work to the first available agent and wait for its reply</span>
<span class="cmd">pilotctl send-message gpu-trainer-1 --wait 30m --data \\
  '{"description":"Train classifier on preprocessed data"}'</span></code></pre>

  <p>If <code>gpu-trainer-1</code> is offline, the send fails and the orchestrator falls back to <code>gpu-trainer-2</code>. Hostnames (and any tags the member list shows) tell the orchestrator what each agent does; a failed send tells it which agents are down.</p>

  <p>With this pattern, a pipeline does not have to be configured in advance. The orchestrator picks peers at runtime, composes the workflow, and adapts when agents join or leave the network.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
  <h2 id="error-handling">Error Handling and Recovery</h2>

  <p>Distributed pipelines fail. Agents crash, networks partition, tasks time out. The pipeline must handle this gracefully:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Message timeout:</strong> If the worker does not respond within a deadline, the orchestrator retries with a different agent.</li>
    <li><strong>Agent offline:</strong> If <code>pilotctl send-message</code> exits with an error (peer unreachable or no reply in time), pick another agent from the member list and retry.</li>
    <li><strong>Partial results:</strong> For fan-out pipelines, collect whatever results arrive within the timeout. Report partial results with a note about which branches completed.</li>
    <li><strong>Idempotent tasks:</strong> Design tasks so they can be safely retried. If the orchestrator is unsure whether a task completed, it can resubmit. The worker should handle duplicate submissions gracefully.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The event-driven model is naturally resilient. If a processing agent crashes mid-pipeline, the events it already published are consumed. The events it has not yet published simply do not trigger the next stage. The event stream does not store or replay events: a replacement agent that subscribes receives events published from then on, so a stage that must not lose work should record what it has processed, or have the upstream stage publish again.</p>
  </section>

  <div class="cta">
    <h3>Build Agent Pipelines</h3>
    <p>Chain, fan-out, or event-drive. Pilot tunnels handle the networking.</p>
    <a href="https://github.com/pilot-protocol/pilotprotocol">View on GitHub</a>
  </div>`;
---
<BlogLayout
  title="Multi-Agent Pipelines Over Encrypted Tunnels"
  description="Chain OpenClaw agents into production pipelines. Every hop encrypted, every agent authenticated, every message reliable."
  date="March 11, 2026"
  tags={["openclaw", "pipelines", "architecture"]}
  canonicalPath="/blog/multi-agent-pipelines-openclaw-encrypted-tunnels"
  bannerImage="/blog/banners/multi-agent-pipelines-openclaw-encrypted-tunnels.webp"
>
  <Fragment set:html={bodyContent} />
</BlogLayout>
